## 3. Controversy & Counter-Intuitive Points

**Conflict 1: The Headcount Calculus**
- Industry says: "AI reduces labor costs — identify what AI can do that humans do, then reduce headcount."
- Blake says: "Compressible work and the person doing it are not the same thing. That person still has knowledge of your business and your customers. Invest in them to do higher-value work."
- Evidence: "There instead is the investment of invest in your people to gain the skills to become better at what they do and take away some of the noise from their jobs."
- Shareable potential: **High**

**Conflict 2: AI Creativity Is Real**
- Industry says: "AI is creative — it generates novel ideas and can be a creative partner."
- Blake says: "Creativity only exists in biology. AI is math all the way down — provably, per OpenAI's own assessment of their model. It can help you explore, but it cannot be the source."
- Evidence: "OpenAI had a paper that they released last year where they talked about having assessed their model and it is math all the way down like it's provable. It's math and there's no creativity in math."
- Shareable potential: **High** — direct counter to a dominant AI vendor narrative.

**Conflict 3: Give Employees AI Access and Productivity Follows**
- Industry says: "Deploy AI tools and your workforce becomes more productive."
- Blake says: "AI's default mode is a golden retriever — endlessly affirming, never critical. Without explicit instruction to give critical feedback, it will just validate your bad ideas. Access without training produces confident mediocrity."
- Evidence: "If you don't basically authorize it to give you critical feedback, it won't. It'll just affirm everything that you do and tell you how brilliant you are."
- Shareable potential: **High**

**Conflict 4: Prompting Is a Simple, Learnable Skill**
- Industry says: "Anyone can prompt AI — it's just asking questions in plain English."
- Blake says: "Prompting is delegation, and delegation is one of the hardest skills in business. Most people are bad at it. If you don't know how to be specific, you'll get the bubbly grapefruit every time."
- Evidence: The delegation analogy — "Hey Dave, can you get me something to drink?" vs. "I am dragging today, can you hook me up with some coffee, little cream, little sugar?"
- Shareable potential: **Medium-High** — the delegation frame is accessible and non-obvious.

**Conflict 5: Passive Video Courseware Is How You Train AI Skills**
- Industry says: "LinkedIn Learning, Udemy, and video-based AI certification courses are the path to AI upskilling."
- Blake says: "It bored me to tears. So much theory, so much vocabulary. You immediately forget all of it. Interactive training with real feedback loops is the only format that actually works."
- Evidence: "I I'm going to be honest, man. It bored me to tears. Like, there was so much theory, so much vocabulary, and I'm like, all okay, I'm just gonna forget all this stuff."
- Shareable potential: **Medium** — speaks directly to L&D buyers.

